From 8f6b169c03a1da9e526111133ad7b3c20eb95e07 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 10:48:39 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Reconciliation credential (Option B), Matica Service side:
 mint a fresh service token per run instead of reusing each entry's stale
 bearer token

Companion to the Inventory API's reconciliation-credential patch (new
PrintAgentServiceAccount, POST api/auth/service-token, the third JWT
scheme). This closes the actual gap that patch enables closing: the
5-minute/30-minute mismatch is gone because reconciliation no longer
depends on any token surviving from the original print request at all.

Services/ReconciliationTokenProvider.cs (new): IReconciliationTokenProvider
exchanges the Printer Agent's own standing credential
(ReconciliationCredentialOptions: ClientId non-secret, ClientSecret via
user-secrets/environment) for a fresh access token via
POST api/auth/service-token. No caching - the job runs at most every 30
minutes plus once at startup, so there's no meaningful cost to minting
fresh every time and no cache-invalidation complexity worth introducing
for a call this infrequent. Parsing logic mirrors PrintFlowClient.ParseAsync
deliberately duplicated rather than extracted into a shared helper - this
is the only other caller, and refactoring an already-shipped, working
file for one new consumer wasn't in scope for this change.

Outbox/OutboxStore.cs: OutboxEntry.BearerToken removed entirely. Not just
a schema cleanup - every pending entry used to be a plaintext file on
disk carrying a live bearer token for however long it stayed queued,
which stops being necessary once reconciliation authenticates with its
own token instead. System.Text.Json ignores unknown properties on
deserialize, so any already-pending entry with a leftover BearerToken
value from before this patch just has it ignored, not treated as an
error.

Outbox/OutboxReconciliationJob.cs: fetches one token at the start of
RunAsync and uses it for every entry that run, rather than each entry's
own stored token. If a mint fails outright, the whole run is skipped
(nothing to reconcile against) and every entry stays queued for the next
attempt - logged distinctly from an ordinary reconciliation failure. If
an individual print-result call fails in a way that looks like the token
itself was the problem (heuristic: the error text mentions 401,
"Unauthenticated", or "token" - see LooksLikeStaleToken's own doc comment
for why this is a heuristic, not a guarantee), the job mints one fresh
token and retries that one entry once - bounded per run, not a loop, so
a persistently invalid credential can't turn one run into an unbounded
retry storm.

Controllers/MachineController.cs: the outbox-write call in Print no
longer sets BearerToken. GetBearerToken() itself is unchanged and still
used for both live backend calls (resolve-for-print, the in-request
print-result attempt) - only the outbox path stopped needing it.

Program.cs: ReconciliationCredentialOptions bound with the same fail-fast
validation convention as every other options class in this service;
IReconciliationTokenProvider registered as a typed HttpClient sharing
InventoryApiOptions.BaseUrl with IPrintFlowClient, since the token
endpoint lives on the same Inventory API. Updated two comments that had
gone stale describing the now-resolved token-expiry limitation, rather
than leaving them contradicting the code next to them.

appsettings.json: new ReconciliationCredential:ClientId (non-secret,
blank placeholder). ClientSecret is not here at all - user-secrets/
environment variable only, same convention as every other secret in
this service.

Re-read the actual current repo state directly from a fresh clone before
making any change, specifically because of the mismatch that happened a
few patches back when a change was built against out-of-date assumptions
about Program.cs - every file touched here was confirmed against its real
current content first, not assumed from an earlier session.

No dotnet SDK or NuGet access in the authoring environment. Verified by:
brace balance across every touched and new file; JSON/XML validity on
appsettings.json, the .csproj, and both resx files; the full-repo
symbol-table cross-check (same four already-known false positives,
nothing new); and a targeted grep confirming zero remaining .BearerToken
references anywhere while GetBearerToken() still correctly serves only
the two live backend calls. Please build locally before merging, and this
needs the companion Inventory API patch (and its migration) applied
first, since this side calls an endpoint that doesn't exist without it.
